\chapter{notes}

\begin{itemize}
  \item The earliest successful Leonardo runs omitted \texttt{keep.in.memory} and \texttt{do.stack}, therefore using the BIOMOD2 defaults \texttt{TRUE/TRUE}. They fitted only 30--35 models per species. Runs with 125--250 models exhausted the approximately 494~GB available on a DCGP node.
  \item The recent audit found no valid completion: the initial three-species campaign was cancelled, one later test was cancelled, and the 2-worker and 112-worker \texttt{TRUE/TRUE} tests failed out of memory at approximately 478 and 481~GiB. Neither produced a \texttt{\_SUCCESS} marker or final timing files.
  \item No historical mixed-storage run was found. After 2 July, all 22 logs with explicit runtime settings used \texttt{FALSE/FALSE}.
  \item Hybrid storage is under controlled test with \texttt{keep.in.memory=FALSE} and \texttt{do.stack=TRUE}. Job \texttt{49842976\_1} runs \emph{Achillea atrata} with 2 workers on \texttt{lrdn4717}; job \texttt{49843592\_1} runs the same species with 4 workers on \texttt{lrdn4682}. Both have 494000~MB, matching CPU billing, and isolated output directories.
  \item Job \texttt{49844162\_1} adds a safer \texttt{FALSE/FALSE} control for the same species, using 4 workers on \texttt{lrdn4376} and isolated output in \texttt{data/output\_disk\_sp1\_4cpu}. The three available nodes are therefore occupied by directly comparable runs.
  \item The plan is to compare completion, elapsed time and MaxRSS across hybrid 2-worker, hybrid 4-worker and disk-backed 4-worker modes. A 112-worker hybrid run was rejected as unnecessarily OOM-prone; the \texttt{FALSE/FALSE} run is the fallback most likely to provide a complete baseline.
  \item With \texttt{--exclusive} removed, \texttt{--mem=0} reserves 494000~MB while CPU allocation and billing match the requested 2 or 4 workers. The previous 112-CPU accounting came from a stale remote script still containing \texttt{--exclusive}. External Slurm mail is configured explicitly with \texttt{ARRAY\_TASKS}, \texttt{END}, \texttt{FAIL} and \texttt{TIME\_LIMIT}; each one-task array should therefore send an individual final notification.
  \item RAM from separate nodes cannot be combined transparently. Multi-node execution would require explicit sharding by model or scenario plus disk-based recombination, so it will be considered only if hybrid storage still fails.
  \item Final Slurm accounting shows that the 4-worker hybrid job \texttt{49843592\_1} failed with exit code 1 after 03:57:25 and the 2-worker hybrid job \texttt{49842976\_1} failed with exit code 1 after 05:44:00. The 4-worker disk-backed job \texttt{49844162\_1} completed with exit code 0 after 22:33:50. Thus only \texttt{FALSE/FALSE} produced a Slurm-level single-species baseline; its \texttt{\_SUCCESS} marker and timing files still need confirmation.
  \item Next, inspect all three logs, resource reports and MaxRSS values. The hybrid failures must not be classified as out-of-memory from exit code 1 alone; their exact failure phase should be established before any refactor or full campaign.
  \item The read-only post-run audit identified the same failure in both hybrid jobs: during clamping-mask construction for the first future scenario, \texttt{mcfork()} returned ``Cannot allocate memory''. Slurm reported batch-step MaxRSS values of 413.22 and 413.80~GiB, versus 260.94~GiB for the completed disk-backed job. Thus the hybrid mode failed from fork-time memory pressure even though Slurm did not assign an \texttt{OUT\_OF\_MEMORY} state.
  \item The disk-backed run is a valid runtime/resource baseline: its \texttt{\_SUCCESS} marker records task 1, 4 BIOMOD workers and completion at 2026-07-20 18:37:21; both timing files exist and list all eight available future scenarios. The species directory contains 1364 non-empty files (7.2~GB), all current and future individual/ensemble projection directories, and 127 model files corresponding to 125 individual models plus two ensembles.
  \item Its 22:33:50 Slurm runtime consists mainly of 67,818.98~s of future projections, with 4,527.93~s formatting, 248.85~s modeling, 137.30~s ensemble modeling, 7,047.56~s current projection and 1,421.92~s current ensemble projection. No fatal application error appears in the log. The non-fatal GLM, MAXNET and ensemble-metric warnings should be audited separately before treating evaluation metrics as scientifically final; they do not invalidate this runtime baseline.
\end{itemize}
